---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CaseStudy from '../../components/CaseStudy.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Pharmacy Layout Redesign - Senior Design | Juan Contreras"
  description="Case study: a two-semester senior design capstone for CornerMed Pharmacy in Dearborn - Juan Contreras led ergonomics (Siemens Jack, RULA from 7 to 3), with team Arena simulation, SLP layouts, and an EOQ ordering change that cut safety-stock holding cost 56.3%."
  current="engineering"
  ogImage="/images/og/pharmacy-redesign.png"
>
  <CaseStudy
    status="Senior design · client project"
    title="Pharmacy Layout Redesign"
    tagline="A two-semester capstone for a working pharmacy in Dearborn: measure everything, simulate the alternatives, and recommend only what the data supports."
    back={{ label: 'All engineering work', href: '/engineering' }}
    meta={[
      { label: 'client', value: 'CornerMed Pharmacy - Dearborn, MI' },
      { label: 'my role', value: 'Ergonomics & human factors lead (plus ethics)' },
      { label: 'team', value: '4 industrial engineering seniors + faculty advisor' },
      { label: 'timeline', value: 'Oct 2022 - Apr 2023 · Senior Design Day, April 14, 2023' },
    ]}
    tags={['DMAIC', 'Siemens Jack', 'RULA', 'Arena', 'SLP', 'EOQ', 'FMEA']}
    image={{
      src: '/images/engineering/pharmacy/jack-model.webp',
      alt: 'The pharmacy modeled in Siemens Jack, with digital manikins at the shelving, packing, and computer stations',
      width: 1250,
      height: 978,
    }}
    imageCaption="The pharmacy rebuilt in Siemens Jack for the posture studies."
  >
    <h2>the client</h2>
    <p>
      CornerMed Pharmacy is an independent retail pharmacy run by its owner-pharmacist and two assistants: consultations,
      order fulfillment, and delivery or pickup. The layout had been set up without a task analysis, which left
      inefficient operations and a backlog of work. The owner was our client and primary contact, and the goal was to
      shorten order processing and make the workspace better for the people in it.
    </p>
    <figure class="fig">
      <img
        src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/storefront.webp"
        alt="CornerMed Pharmacy storefront in Dearborn, Michigan"
        width="1200"
        height="900"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>CornerMed Pharmacy, Dearborn - the senior design client.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>approach</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Define:</strong> site visits, project charter, scope (layout and in-store tasks, not delivery).</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measure:</strong> drew the layout to scale, measured every station and shelf height, and timed
        interarrivals, processing, and walking speed (56.32 in/s).
      </li>
      <li><strong>Analyze:</strong> observation and interviews, a SIPOC diagram, and a fishbone diagram.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Improve:</strong> SLP and graph-based layouts, Arena simulation, Siemens Jack posture analysis, and lean
        inventory planning.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Control:</strong> recommendations to the owner, with an FMEA for the risks of each change.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>my part: ergonomics</h2>
    <p>
      I led the human factors work. We focused on the three most-visited stations - packing, computer, and shelving -
      and designed for adjustability across the population, from a 5th-percentile female to a 95th-percentile male.
      Before trusting Jack, I scored one real posture by hand and the same posture in Jack; the results matched closely,
      so Jack carried the rest of the analysis.
    </p>
    <p>
      RULA scores upper-body posture risk from 1 (acceptable) to 7 (change immediately). Current vs. proposed, for the
      smallest and largest users:
    </p>
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>station</th>
          <th>what was wrong</th>
          <th>change</th>
          <th>5th % female</th>
          <th>95th % male</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Shelving</strong></td>
          <td>Overreaching to the top shelves - heavy upper-arm and neck strain</td>
          <td>Three-level step stool; most-used stock moved to standing work height</td>
          <td><strong>7 → 3</strong></td>
          <td><strong>6 → 3</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Packing</strong></td>
          <td>Upper-arm strain for shorter workers, neck strain for taller ones, standing fatigue</td>
          <td>Height-adjustable workstation and anti-fatigue mats</td>
          <td><strong>5 → 2</strong></td>
          <td><strong>5 → 2</strong></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Computer</strong></td>
          <td>Unsupported legs; a fixed chair strained taller users' trunks</td>
          <td>Height-adjustable chair with foot support</td>
          <td><strong>3 → 2</strong></td>
          <td><strong>4 → 3</strong></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="fig-pair">
      <figure class="fig">
        <img
          src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/rula-shelving-before.webp"
          alt="Siemens Jack RULA analysis of a 5th-percentile female reaching to the top shelf: grand score 7, changes required immediately"
          width="970"
          height="780"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Shelving, current: RULA 7 - change immediately.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="fig">
        <img
          src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/rula-shelving-after.webp"
          alt="Siemens Jack RULA analysis of the same reach using a step stool: grand score 3"
          width="892"
          height="770"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Shelving with the step stool: RULA 3.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="fig-pair">
      <figure class="fig">
        <img
          src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/rula-packing-before.webp"
          alt="Siemens Jack RULA analysis at the current packing table: grand score 5, changes required soon"
          width="1000"
          height="673"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Packing, current: RULA 5 - change soon.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="fig">
        <img
          src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/rula-packing-after.webp"
          alt="Siemens Jack RULA analysis at a height-adjustable packing workstation: grand score 2, acceptable"
          width="1000"
          height="795"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Height-adjustable packing station: RULA 2 - acceptable.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <p>We wrote down the tradeoffs too, because every fix has them:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Adjustable stations only help if workers adjust them - they need a short training and a habit.</li>
      <li>A step stool can become a tripping hazard, and it does nothing for reaching below the knee.</li>
      <li>Moving inventory means relearning where things are.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Against those, the cost of mats, a stool, and adjustable chairs and desks was small next to the benefit to comfort
      and burnout.
    </p>

    <h2>team results: layout and simulation</h2>
    <p>
      Teammates led the facilities layout, Arena simulation, and lean and cost-benefit work; I contributed to the team's
      DMAIC and recommendations. We built two layout alternatives from a from-to chart of trips between stations - one
      with SLP, one graph-based.
    </p>
    <figure class="fig">
      <img
        src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/layout-alternatives.webp"
        alt="Graph-based layout diagram next to the SLP block layout drawn to the stations' space requirements"
        width="1560"
        height="1013"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>Graph-based method to an SLP block layout sized to each station's space needs.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The Arena model used measured distances between all 9 stations (81 pairs) with workers as transporters at the
      measured walking speed. Eight variants - original, SLP, and graph-based layouts, each with and without an added
      packing table, plus defined roles - were compared with 2-sample t-tests in Output Analyzer, and Process Analyzer
      tried staffing changes up to an all-pharmacist team.
    </p>
    <figure class="fig">
      <img
        src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/arena-model.webp"
        alt="Overview of the Arena model: order processing stations and the in-person reception area"
        width="1232"
        height="486"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>The Arena model, organized by station and function.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="story">
      <h3>The best answer was "don't move anything"</h3>
      <p>
        The SLP layout looked better in the raw report, but the t-tests found no statistically significant difference on
        any measure, and staffing changes made no difference either. The pharmacy is small enough that, at the measured
        walking speed, a new layout saves at most about 4 seconds. We recommended keeping the original layout - there was
        no justification for the cost of changing it - and put the effort where the data pointed instead.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>team results: inventory</h2>
    <p>
      Moving the demand assumption from the third to the second standard deviation cut assumed daily demand by
      <strong>10.4%</strong>. The EOQ model then moved ordering from <strong>423 items twice a week</strong> to
      <strong>185 items four to five times a week</strong>, saving <strong>56.3%</strong> of the safety stock's holding
      cost.
    </p>
    <figure class="fig fig-narrow">
      <img
        src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/eoq.webp"
        alt="EOQ cost curves showing the old order quantity near 423 items and the new optimum near 185 items"
        width="1180"
        height="962"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>Total cost vs. order quantity: old (red) and new (green) order sizes.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>recommendations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep the original layout - no significant gain from moving it</li>
      <li>Make workstation heights adjustable, add anti-fatigue mats, provide a step stool</li>
      <li>Order 185 items four to five times a week, with a 10.4% lower assumed demand</li>
      <li>Repeat DMAIC at a narrower scale - order picking and packing - where the time actually goes</li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="fig">
      <a href="/images/engineering/pharmacy/poster.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <img
          src="/images/engineering/pharmacy/poster.webp"
          alt="Senior Design Day poster: Pharmacy Layout Redesign and Efficiency Improvement"
          width="2400"
          height="1364"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>Our Senior Design Day poster (April 14, 2023) - open full size.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>what it shows</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Ergonomic analysis across the population, with the tool verified before it was trusted</li>
      <li>DMAIC on a real client with real constraints</li>
      <li>The discipline to recommend "no change" when the statistics say so</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseStudy>
</Base>
